==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|pungens}} (stem {{m|la||pungent-}}), present participle of {{m|la|pungo||to sting}}. {{doublet|en|poignant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|pŭnjənt}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈpʌnd͡ʒənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-pungent.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having a [[strong]] [[odor]] that [[sting]]s the nose; {{n-g|said especially of [[acidic]] or [[spicy]] substances}}.
#: {{ux|en|I accidentally dropped the bottle of ammonia and after few seconds, a very '''pungent''' stench could be detected.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=February|title=Notes and News: Lynton & Barnstaple Remains|journal=Railway Magazine|page=136|text=Pilton Yard, the Lynton & Barnstaple headquarters, has been taken over by a fur trading firm, and would-be trespassers to the old engine-shed are turned back by the '''pungent''' odour of heaps of carcases.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=w:Paul Chadwick|title=Concrete: American Christmas|publisher=Dark Horse Books
|passage=I can almost smell the fir scent... resinous, '''pungent'''.}}
# Having a [[strong]] [[taste]] that [[sting]]s the tongue; {{n-g|said especially of [[hot]] (spicy) food, which has a strong and sharp or bitter taste}}.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Stinging; [[acerbic]].
#: {{ux|en|The critic gave a '''pungent''' review.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} Having a [[sharp]] and [[stiff]] [[point]].

